  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at height 0.5 × loss over x, z from −2 to 2. x is the steep direction, so overshoot appears there first: learning rates above 2/3 make the x swings grow until the run diverges.",
    "Each step moves the ball by −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise is Gaussian, centred on zero, with size 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch b: largest at 1, exactly zero at 64.",
    "Both arrows are steps (learning rate × slope estimate), drawn at 3 times their true length, one scale for both. The purple one is the move the ball takes next; the dark one is the step the full 64 points would give.",
    "Diverged: the ball leaves the drawn bowl or the loss passes 6; the run stops and the path is drawn at the rim. Finished: 40 steps taken. The trail holds the start point plus one point per step.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed by run and step number, so Reset replays the same sequence; New run uses the next noise values and keeps the finished path as a faded ghost.",
    "Five updates per second while running. With noise, single steps can raise the loss; it falls only on average."
  ]
}